def read(p):return json.loads(p.read_text())
def dump(p,v):p.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True);p.write_text(json.dumps(v,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
def stamp(x,cids,block=False):
 x['claims_seen']={c:1 for c in cids}
 if not block:x['qa_status']='review_required'
 x['authored_hash']=hashlib.sha256(_artefact_text(x).encode()).hexdigest()
FACTS={
'01':['Weathering breaks down or chemically alters rock in place; erosion wears away and removes material through an agent.','Rock splitting without evidence of material removal does not by itself establish erosion.'],
'02':['Mechanical weathering fragments rock without changing the minerals, whereas chemical weathering alters susceptible minerals through reactions.','Organisms contribute mechanical and chemical weathering effects, so biological activity can overlap the physical/chemical classification.'],
'02-01':['Physical weathering changes rock size or shape without requiring chemical mineral change.','Unchanged mineral composition in smaller fragments supports a mechanical-breakdown interpretation.'],
'02-02':['Chemical weathering changes minerals through reactions, including hydrolysis, carbonation and oxidation.','Clay replacing feldspar provides evidence of alteration, whereas water entering a crack alone does not establish hydrolysis.'],
'02-03':['Roots growing in existing joints can widen them mechanically and help loosen rock.','Biological weathering can involve chemical activity as well as mechanical effects; a nearby plant alone does not identify the mechanism.'],
'03':['Moisture and temperature patterns interact with mineral composition, jointing and exposure to influence weathering.','A comparison can isolate one factor only when relevant other conditions are controlled; the effects remain process-specific.'],
'03-01':['Warm moist conditions generally favour many chemical-weathering reactions, while water availability limits water-dependent reactions.','Freeze-thaw effectiveness depends on water and suitable freezing/thawing conditions, not simply the lowest air temperature.'],
'03-02':['Mineral composition affects susceptibility to chemical reactions, while open joints increase access for weathering agents.','Porosity is pore-space proportion; permeability concerns fluid passage through connected openings, so the two are not interchangeable.'],
'81':['Hydrolysis is chemical alteration of susceptible minerals by reactions involving water.','Feldspar alteration to clay illustrates hydrolysis; mechanical splitting by water or ice does not demonstrate that chemical change.'],
'82':['Dissolved carbon dioxide forms weak carbonic acid in water, which can dissolve calcium carbonate in limestone.','Carbonation can enlarge limestone joints without freezing; a wider crack alone is insufficient to identify the chemical mechanism.'],
'83':['Oxidation involves oxygen reacting with susceptible minerals, including iron-bearing minerals forming iron oxides.','New iron oxides at an exposed rim provide stronger evidence than brown colour alone.'],
'84':['Freeze-thaw weathering can occur when water enters cracks and freezes, with repeated effective cycles widening joints.','Water supply, accessible cracks and freezing/thawing conditions distinguish this physical mechanism from thermal exfoliation.'],
'85':['Exfoliation involves detachment of outer rock sheets; thermal expansion/contraction can contribute to crack growth and peeling.','Exfoliation can have additional causes such as unloading, so surface appearance alone does not establish a unique thermal history.']}
CARDS={
'001':('Distinguish between weathering and erosion.','Weathering breaks down or alters rock in place. Erosion involves wearing away and removal by an agent. The key difference is in-place breakdown or alteration versus agent-driven wearing and removal.',['1 mark for weathering breakdown/alteration; 1 for erosion by an agent; 1 for the explicit difference concerning removal.']),
'002':('Suggest why the observations support weathering at A and erosion at B: at A, a cracked block has split but fragments remain below it; at B, flowing water wears the face and carries fragments downstream.','A demonstrates breakdown in place because the fragments remain. B supplies a moving-water agent and evidence of wearing/removal. The same rock could experience both processes at different stages.',['1 mark for applying the split-block evidence to breakdown; 1 for the given retained fragments as a reason for in-place change; 1 for the wearing/removal evidence at B.']),
'003':('Describe how physical, chemical and biological weathering change rock, giving a mechanism for each.','Physical weathering breaks rock without changing its minerals, for example ice-related pressure in cracks. Chemical weathering changes minerals through reactions, such as feldspar alteration. Biological activity can widen joints through root growth or contribute chemical effects.',['1 mark for the physical breakdown step; 1 for the chemical reaction and changed-mineral result; 1 for an organism-linked action and its effect.']),
'004':('Describe what these observations indicate: A has smaller fragments with unchanged minerals; B has clay replacing feldspar; C has a thickening root inside a widening joint.','A supports physical breakdown. B provides evidence of chemical alteration. C supports biological activity producing a mechanical effect by widening the joint.',['1 mark for A’s unchanged-mineral feature; 1 for B’s mineral-replacement evidence; 1 for interpreting C’s root/joint relationship as biological and mechanical.']),
'005':('Describe the weathering evidence in a specimen that has split into smaller pieces but retains the same mineral composition; the fragments remain beside the original block.','The smaller pieces show physical breakdown. Unchanged minerals support a mechanical rather than chemical interpretation. Retained fragments show in-place change without evidence of removal.',['1 mark for the fragmentation feature; 1 for using the unchanged-mineral evidence; 1 for the retained-fragment evidence and its in-place interpretation.']),
'006':('Describe the evidence for chemical weathering in this record: original feldspar is partly replaced by clay minerals, and altered grains remain within the outcrop.','The mineral replacement shows chemical change rather than a change of size alone. Feldspar alteration to clay is consistent with hydrolysis. The grains remaining within the outcrop support alteration in place.',['1 mark for the changed-mineral feature; 1 for interpreting the feldspar/clay evidence as hydrolysis; 1 for the in-place observation.']),
'007':('Describe the biological-weathering evidence: repeated photographs show a root thickening inside an existing joint while the joint widens; the mineral composition is unchanged.','Root growth is associated with joint widening. The widening indicates a mechanical effect. Unchanged minerals give no evidence of chemical alteration in this record.',['1 mark for the root/joint relationship; 1 for using the widening observation as a physical effect; 1 for correctly interpreting the unchanged-mineral evidence.']),
'008':('Explain how climate and jointing can combine to influence chemical weathering of susceptible rock.','Moisture provides water for reactions, and warmer conditions can increase reaction rates where water is available. Open joints let water reach more surfaces, increasing contact with susceptible minerals. Mineral alteration can then weaken the rock, though the response depends on its composition and conditions.',['1 mark for moisture as a causal factor; 1 for the temperature–reaction link with water available; 1 for joint access increasing contact; 1 for the linked mineral-change/weakening effect.']),
'009':('Describe the interpretation supported by this controlled comparison: same rock, sample size, temperature and time; A receives water and develops alteration products, B stays dry and develops none detected.','Alteration is detected in the moist sample, not the dry one. The stated controls make water supply the changed factor. The pattern supports water helping the reaction, but does not test a temperature difference.',['1 mark for the observed moist/dry pattern; 1 for the controlled comparison as evidence concerning water; 1 for retaining the limitation that temperature was not varied.']),
'010':('Name the climatic requirement, besides suitable temperature changes, for freeze-thaw weathering inside rock cracks.','Available water entering the cracks.',['1 mark for the feature named: water supply/access.']),
'011':('Name one rock feature that gives water access to internal surfaces and can increase weathering.','Open joints or connected fractures.',['1 mark for the name open joints/connected fractures.']),
'012':('Define hydrolysis as a weathering process.','Chemical alteration of susceptible minerals through reactions involving water.',['1 mark for the definition’s essential chemical mineral change involving water; water entry alone is insufficient.']),
'013':('Suggest which observation gives stronger evidence of hydrolysis and why: A shows water in a crack with no detected mineral change; B shows clay replacing feldspar after water exposure.','B is stronger evidence because it establishes alteration of feldspar to a new mineral product. A demonstrates water access but not the chemical change needed to identify hydrolysis.',['1 mark for selecting B from the given evidence; 1 for the reason involving feldspar-to-clay mineral change; 1 for explaining why A’s water access alone is insufficient.']),
'014':('Describe the sequence of carbonation affecting jointed limestone.','Carbon dioxide dissolves in water to form weak carbonic acid. This water enters joints and reacts with calcium carbonate. Dissolved products are removed in solution, enlarging openings over time.',['1 mark for the first carbon-dioxide/weak-acid step; 1 for contact and reaction with limestone carbonate; 1 for solution removal and enlarged joints as the result.']),
'015':('Describe the carbonation sequence in the supplied diagram, using its gas, water path and limestone joint.','Carbon dioxide enters the water, forming weak carbonic acid. The water follows the limestone joint and reacts with calcium carbonate. Dissolved material leaves in the water, so the joint enlarges over time.',['1 mark for the gas-to-weak-acid relationship; 1 for the water-path/contact evidence; 1 for the dissolution and enlarging-joint pattern.']),
'016':('Describe how oxidation can weather iron-bearing rock.','Oxygen and moisture reach susceptible iron-bearing minerals. Reaction produces iron oxides. The mineral alteration can weaken the material and contribute to breakdown.',['1 mark for the first oxygen-access step; 1 for reaction/new iron-oxide formation; 1 for alteration and weakening as a result.']),
'017':('Describe the oxidation evidence: an exposed rock rim contains newly identified iron oxides, while its protected interior retains the original iron-bearing minerals.','The altered minerals are concentrated at the exposed rim. The interior is less altered in the stated record. Newly identified iron oxides provide chemical evidence for oxidation rather than colour change alone.',['1 mark for the exposed-rim feature; 1 for the rim/interior contrast using the data; 1 for interpreting the new mineral evidence as oxidation.']),
'018':('Describe how repeated freeze-thaw conditions can break jointed rock.','Water enters a crack and freezes under suitable conditions. Ice-related expansion/pressure stresses the sides of the joint. Thawing allows further water entry, and repeated effective cycles can widen the joint until fragments loosen.',['1 mark for the first water-entry/freezing step; 1 for ice-related pressure on joint walls; 1 for repeated cycles and fragment loosening as the result.']),
'019':('Describe the process represented in the supplied freeze-thaw sequence, using the water, ice and joint-width observations.','Water enters the joint; freezing produces ice-related stress against its walls. Repeated thawing and refreezing permit further widening. The final loosened fragment is a result of physical breakdown, without requiring mineral change.',['1 mark for the water-to-ice sequence using diagram evidence; 1 for the repeated-widening pattern; 1 for interpreting the loosened fragment as physical breakdown.']),
'020':('Describe how thermal stresses can contribute to exfoliation.','The exposed surface heats and expands differently from the interior. Cooling produces contraction and changing stresses. Repeated stress can grow surface-parallel fractures, allowing outer sheets to detach.',['1 mark for the first differential heating/expansion step; 1 for cooling/contraction and stress change; 1 for fracture growth and sheet detachment as the result.']),
'021':('Describe the thermal-exfoliation sequence in the supplied diagram, using the outer sheet, inner rock and changing-temperature stages.','The exposed outer material warms and expands relative to the interior, then cools and contracts. Repeated stresses help surface-parallel cracks grow. An outer sheet detaches, producing the observed peeling form.',['1 mark for the heating/cooling relationship from diagram evidence; 1 for the surface-parallel crack-growth pattern; 1 for linking the detached outer sheet to peeling.']),
'022':('Explain the error in saying “a rock split, so it must be erosion”. A shows split fragments remaining in place; B shows flowing water wearing the rock and removing pieces. Use the pair as a test.','The claim confuses breakdown with removal. A demonstrates weathering in place; B additionally establishes an erosional agent wearing and removing material. The distinguishing test is evidence of removal, not splitting alone.',['1 mark for identifying the confusion/error; 1 for the correct distinction applied through the A/B test.']),
'023':('Explain why water inside a crack does not by itself prove hydrolysis. Which would discriminate better: wet unchanged rock or clay replacing feldspar?','The error confuses water access with a mineral-changing reaction. Clay replacing feldspar is the stronger test because it establishes chemical alteration; wet unchanged rock does not.',['1 mark for the water-access/reaction confusion; 1 for the correct mineral-change test selecting clay replacement.']),
'024':('Explain the error in treating these two sequences as the same process: A = water enters a joint, freezes and stresses its walls; B = heating/cooling stresses outer rock sheets until they peel.','The error confuses ice-related pressure in cracks with thermal stresses in the rock. A is freeze-thaw; B is thermal exfoliation. Test for water/freezing versus differential heating/cooling and peeling.',['1 mark for identifying the mechanism confusion; 1 for the correct distinction and discriminating test applied to A and B.'])
}
